  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an interchange station?",
      "a": "An interchange is a station where two or more lines meet, so passengers can change from one line to another without leaving the system.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you change lines on the metro?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do several metro lines go through the city centre?",
      "a": "Most journeys start or end in the centre, so planners send several lines through it. That spreads the crowds and means most places are one change away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is the city centre so well connected?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do metro trains run so close together?",
      "a": "Metros have their own tracks with no level crossings, and modern signalling tracks every train continuously, so trains can safely follow each other every two or three minutes or less.",
      "asks": [
        "How often do metro trains come?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are some metro trains driverless?",
      "a": "On a fully separate track, computers can control speed, stopping and spacing very precisely. Platform screen doors keep people off the track, so many new metros run without a driver.",
      "asks": [
        "Can trains drive themselves?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a metro depot for?",
      "a": "The depot is where trains are parked, cleaned, inspected and repaired, usually overnight or between rush hours.",
      "asks": [
        "Where do metro trains go at night?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The London Underground, opened in 1863, was the world's first underground railway.",
    "Many modern metros, such as the Copenhagen Metro, run fully driverless trains.",
    "Platform screen doors keep people off the track and need trains to stop in exactly the right place."
